Faster redrawing in OPL
Here is a technique for getting:

a) Faster redrawing in OPL
b) Redrawing the screen while showing something else
c) Smooth animation

all using just the standard OPL window and OPL graphics
commands. This is very useful for having instantly
appearing or changing screens.

To do this, use the following section of code:

call($108d,wid%)
<your redraw code here>
call($04d6)
call($008d)
call($158d,wid%)
testevent

where wid% is obtained using ginfo. Your redraw code can
be as long/complicated as you like - the user sees nothing
happening - they just see the previously drawn screen.
You can even use gcls to redraw completely.

It works as follows:

call($108d,wid%) is wBeginRedrawWin.

Any following OPL graphics commands will be directed to
the backup bitmap of the window.

call($04d6) flushes the graphics buffer.

call($008d) is wEndRedraw. Returns drawing to normal.

call($158d,wid%) calls wInvalidateWin. This makes the
window server copy the backup bitmap (to which you have
just drawn) to the main window. The copying of the backup
bitmap wont occur until you use testevent (or get, getevent).

A simple demo follows. It draws a pattern while displaying
the please wait. It is far quicker than drawing directly.

I'll upload a file to cix shortly giving full details
of this and other techniques for fast graphics.

PROC demo:
 global wid%
 
 defaultwin 1
 
 wid%=getid:
 
 gat 0,80
 gfont 12
 gprintb "Please wait ... drawing screen",480,3
 
 call($108d,wid%)
 drawpat: 
 call($04d6)
 call($008d)
 call($158d,wid%)
 get
ENDP
 
PROC drawpat:
 local x%,y%
 ggrey 2
 gcls
 ggrey 0
 x%=0
 y%=0
 do
   gat x%,0
   glineto 480,y%
   glineto 480-x%,160
   glineto 0,160-y%
   glineto x%,0
   y%=y%+8
   x%=x%+24
 until x%=480
ENDP

ENDP

PROC getid:
 rem return window handle
 local gi%(31)
 
 ginfo gi%()
  
 return gi%(31)
ENDP

Hope this is useful to someone :-)

